[size=5][b]PalForge — add new pals, items and buildings to Palworld[/b][/size]

[b]This is a modder's tool, not a gameplay mod.[/b] On its own it changes nothing you can see. What it does is let someone else add a pal, an item, a building, a skill, a status effect, a sound, a model or a UI panel in a short Lua file instead of a large one — and react to what the game does, without writing any plumbing.

If you are here to play, you want a mod [i]built on[/i] PalForge, not PalForge.

[line]

[size=4][b]What you can add[/b][/size]

Eight kinds of thing, and every one is the same shape — call it to declare, get a handle back:

[list]
[*][b]Pal[/b] — creatures, with a mesh, a material, skills and a lifecycle
[*][b]Item[/b] — things, with what they restore and what happens when they are used
[*][b]Building[/b] — structures, with per-structure state that survives a reload
[*][b]Skill[/b] — active moves and passives
[*][b]Effect[/b] — status ailments, including the game's own 38
[*][b]Audio[/b] — from a 1957-entry catalog of the game's own sounds
[*][b]Mesh[/b] — models and materials
[*][b]UI[/b] — panels mounted inside Palworld's own interface
[/list]

[code]Item{
    id       = "mypack:Potion",
    name     = "Healing Potion",
    restores = { hpRate = 0.25 },
    events   = {
        onUse = function(self, ctx)
            Audio.get("AKE_Heal"):play()
        end,
    },
}[/code]

[b]⚠️ How a genuinely NEW entity works, stated plainly.[/b] Lua cannot write a row into Palworld's data tables, so a brand-new item, creature or build object needs [b]PalSchema[/b] to create the row (search Nexus for it, or see its GitHub linked at the bottom). PalForge does everything after that — behaviour, events, saved state — and its ids are spelled to be exactly what PalSchema writes ([font=Courier New]mypack:Potion[/font] becomes the row [font=Courier New]mypack_Potion[/font]).

To give something the game [i]already has[/i] new behaviour, PalForge alone is enough.

[line]

[size=4][b]And the events are already wired[/b][/size]

Twenty-one channels, fed by twenty-two hooks into the game itself. You declare a handler; it fires. There is no polling loop to write and no hook to register.

[code]Building{
    id = "CampFire",                        -- the game's own campfire
    events = {
        onRightClick = function(self, ctx)  -- fires in a real save
            self.state.uses = self.state.uses + 1
            self:save()                     -- survives a reload
        end,
    },
}[/code]

Placement, right-click, load, tick, removal · craft, obtain, use, discard · spawned, damaged, death, captured · skill activation and passives. Each one was found by hooking the game and watching it fire, not by guessing at a name — and a channel that has no source behind it on this build [i]says so[/i] rather than staying quiet.

[size=4][b]⚠️ SINGLE-PLAYER ONLY[/b][/size]

[b]PalForge targets single-player Palworld. Dedicated servers and co-op guests are not supported and are not tested.[/b] There is no replication layer, and the item, spawn and event routes are all client-authoritative. A pack may appear to work for the host and do nothing for anyone else.

This is a deliberate scope decision, not an oversight. Please do not report multiplayer behaviour as a bug — the answer is this paragraph.

[line]

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Palworld[/b] — measured against [b]v1.0.2.101103[/b] (Steam, Win64). Other builds will probably work; that is the one every capability was tested on, and the startup line prints both the build it was measured on and the build you are running, so a mismatch is visible rather than mysterious.
[*][b]UE4SS[/b] with the Lua mod loader. If you already run other Palworld Lua mods, you have it.
[*][b]PalSchema[/b] — [i]required to add NEW content[/i], optional to extend what the game already has. See the note above.
[*][b]CheatManagerEnablerMod[/b] — [i]optional[/i]. PalForge builds a cheat manager itself for spawning; the enabler only helps the item helpers (give / take / unlock).
[/list]

[line]

[size=4][b]Install[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Download the zip.
[*]Drop the [b]PalForge[/b] folder into [font=Courier New]Pal/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/Mods/[/font].
[*]Start the game. Check [font=Courier New]ue4ss/UE4SS.log[/font] for a line beginning [font=Courier New]PalForge v0.3.0 starting[/font].
[/list]

The zip contains one folder, named PalForge, and the shape of the zip is the install instruction. Nothing is written to your save by installing it.

[line]

[size=4][b]What actually works[/b][/size]

Every capability below was confirmed in a real save, not inferred from a header:

[list]
[*][b]Buildings[/b] — placement, right-click, load, tick, removal, and per-structure state that survives a reload
[*][b]Items[/b] — give, take, count, and the craft / obtain / use / discard events
[*][b]Pals[/b] — spawn at a position, read and write skills, mesh and material overrides
[*][b]Skills[/b] — activation and passive equip / unequip events
[*][b]Status effects[/b] — the game's own 38 ailments, applied and removed
[*][b]Audio[/b] — a 1957-entry catalog of the game's own sounds
[*][b]UI[/b] — panels mounted into Palworld's own layout, with declared keys and mouse buttons
[/list]

And the honest other half, because a framework that oversells is worse than one that is small:

[list]
[*][b]A pack cannot ship its own mesh, texture, sound or material[/b] as a game asset. It can reference any of the game's own, and it can load geometry from an .obj on disk. Custom textures are wired but unproven.
[*][b]Some things the engine simply does not expose[/b] — there is no "structure was destroyed" event and no "skill hit something" event on this build, and PalForge says so in the log rather than pretending.
[/list]

[line]

[size=4][b]Does it touch my save?[/b][/size]

[b]PalForge never writes to Palworld's save file.[/b] Its own state — which structures a pack is tracking, and whatever that pack chose to remember — lives in plain JSON under [font=Courier New]ue4ss/Mods/PalForge/state/[/font], one folder per save, one file per mod. Delete the mod folder and all of it is gone with no effect on your world.

What a [i]pack built on PalForge[/i] does is a different question — an item it gives you goes into your inventory, which is in your save, and that item's row exists because that pack is installed. Ask its author.

[line]

[size=4][b]Performance[/b][/size]

The building runtime does not poll. It watches Palworld's own events, and it only enumerates the world when something happened that could have changed it. Measured in a base with 623 structures: [b]2.2 ms per sweep, with 4 of 120 sweeps doing any work at all[/b]. A session with no building pack installed pays nothing — it declines to run entirely.

[line]

[size=4][b]For modders[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Full documentation, in English, Japanese and Chinese: [url=https://dr-mikan.github.io/PalForge/]dr-mikan.github.io/PalForge[/url]
[*]Source, issues and the complete engineering record: [url=https://github.com/dr-mikan/PalForge]github.com/dr-mikan/PalForge[/url]
[*]MIT licensed. Build on it, fork it, ship it.
[/list]

Editor completion works out of the box — PalForge ships a generated [font=Courier New]types.lua[/font], so a Lua language server knows every field of every declaration and every method of every handle.

[line]

[size=4][b]Credits[/b][/size]

Built on [url=https://github.com/UE4SS-RE/RE-UE4SS]UE4SS[/url]. Namespaced content ids follow the convention [url=https://github.com/Okaetsu/PalSchema]PalSchema[/url] writes into the game's data tables.
